                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="385-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Electro-Voice Blue Raven</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The EV Raven is a stylish cardioid dynamic instrument and vocal microphone. </p><p>The swivel-mount head and compact design make the mic easy to position.</p><p>The case design is similar to the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Electro-Voice/Cardinal">Cardinal</a>, a condenser mic from the same EV/Blue series. Though in the case of the Cardinal, the body of the case is made of cherry stained hardwood (The Raven is metal).</p><blockquote><p><cite>SoundOnSound</cite></p><p>&#8230;to give good speech intelligibility &#8230; there&#8217;s a subtle presence hump at around 5kHz and another peak at around 12kHz before the response falls away towards 16kHz&#8230;</p></blockquote><p>The Raven was designed by <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Blue-Microphones">Blue</a> around the time that <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/press-releases/telex-communications-takes-on-distribution-of-blue-microphones/">Telex Communications, parent of Electrovoice, signed a distribution deal with Blue</a>. The original branding on this mic was &#8220;EV/Blue Raven.&#8221; Since Blue and Telex have parted ways, Electrovoice retained the rights to the Raven, and are updating the branding to reflect this. The new name is simply Raven.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Electro-Voice/Raven">Electro-Voice Blue Raven</a> is also known as: EV Blue Raven, Blue Raven.</p><p>The mic was released in 2005.</p>
